            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "I'd be curious to see if it's noticeable. Did you get any numbers?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "George Liu",
                        "email": "liuxiwei@ieisystem.com",
                        "username": "lxwinspur"
                    },
                    "message": "I did a quick benchmark using std::chrono with 10M iterations on a 1000-char string and a short prefix.\nThe results were:\n```\nboost::starts_with: ~0.027 s\nstd::starts_with: ~0.020 s\n```\nSo std::starts_with is around 20\u201325% faster.\nIn real code, the difference will usually be negligible unless it\u2019s in a very hot path. My main motivation for switching was to reduce the Boost dependency and use the standard library API."
                }
            ],
